# Every supply with nowhere to put its excess that states all its unserved shares, beside what its
# own figures say went unserved. One row per layer examined, `violates` where the exposure exceeds
# what anybody admits.
#
# A figure nobody measured
#   Nobody files the exposure. It is the most the filer's own demand and nameplate allow: the
#   largest demand against the smallest supply. What could have gone unserved is at most what the
#   buffers can absorb plus what the filer admits went unserved, and here the buffers absorb
#   nothing by the filer's own measurement. So the whole exposure has to be accounted for in the
#   holder rows.
#
# `absorbable` is read, never assumed
#   It is the sum across all the layer's buffers, and it is zero for every layer here. The detail
#   prints it rather than a literal 0, which would speak for the capacity buffer alone.
#
# Read at the worst case
#   The exposure is the worst case, and it is compared with the largest unserved shares. Demand and
#   supply do not peak together (the week demand ran highest is not the week supply ran lowest), so
#   anything added up across the demand range would report a week that never happened.
#
# An unstated unserved share suspends the comparison
#   Those layers are left out rather than passed. Borrowing a neighbouring rule's filter on a
#   positive exposure instead would be a different population, so its count would not be a count
#   of what this rule checks.
#
# Unserved shares in another unit are not compared
#   A share quoted in a unit other than the layer's is not a smaller share; it is not a share of
#   this remainder at all, and setting it against the exposure is a comparison with no answer.
#   arithmetic/exposure_bounded.sqlc calls those layers `not comparable`, and this rule leaves them
#   out rather than judging them.
#
# The unserved total is joined, never filled in
#   An outer join would invent rows: filling a missing unserved total with zero gives a layer with
#   no unserved holder a zero nobody filed, and brings it into the rows examined. Each such layer
#   reads an exposure of zero, so the rule would pass on every one of them without being able to
#   fire, and reports/coverage.sqlc would count them as coverage. arithmetic/exposure_bounded.sqlc,
#   the site this rule guards, joins the same way, so the rule and its site agree on their
#   population.
-- every slack on the layer accounted for and empty, against entries/unserved_totals.sqlc.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT e.filing, e.layer,
           e.exposure > e.unserved + 1e-9 AS violates,
           format('exposure %s, absorbable %s, unserved %s',
                  e.exposure, e.absorbable, e.unserved) AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT x.filing, x.layer, x.exposure, x.absorbable, u.unserved_high AS unserved
        FROM      (
            :compose(layers/unabsorbed_exposure.sqlc)
        ) x
        JOIN      (
            :compose(entries/unserved_totals.sqlc)
        ) u USING (filing, layer)
        WHERE u.unstated = 0
          AND u.share_units = ARRAY[x.unit]
    ) e
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'exposure_unaccounted'
